/**

@page page_peano4_particles_realisation Particle storage and exchange

\tableofcontents

\li \subpage page_peano4_particles_realisation_particle_lift_flavours

@page page_peano4_particles_realisation_particle_lift_flavours Particle-set storage

The generator peano4.particles.ParticleSet selects the C++ container
used by a generated particle set. Every set stores pointers to particles and
inherits the common peano4::particles::ParticleSet lifecycle.

# Particle allocation

Set storage and particle allocation are separate choices. The allocation puts
every particle on the heap on its own.

The rank-local Peano vertex pool stores one generated particle-set object per
relevant vertex slot. Each object owns its pointer container, while particle
objects follow the selected allocation policy.

# Container operations

Generated sets provide standard iteration and size operations. `clear()`
removes the pointers without deleting the particles. `deleteParticle()` and
`deleteParticles()` remove the pointers and release the corresponding particle
objects through the configured allocation policy.

Deleting a particle invalidates every other pointer to it. Callers therefore
delete only after the particle has left all vertex, boundary, and sieve
containers that can reference it.

# Persistent storage and exchange

The generated `load()` and `store()` predicates select whether a vertex's
particle set participates in a traversal event. `send()` and
`receiveAndMerge()` implement the data-type-specific boundary policy.

Particle sets declare that they do not support subtree-task replication. A step
using them traverses each rank serially, so
peano4::stacks::SendReceiveContext::IntraRank is invalid for their generated
merge routine. Rank-boundary exchange uses
peano4::stacks::SendReceiveContext::InterRank.

peano4::communication::CommunicationManager transports the registered
container payload. The container serialisation creates independent incoming
particle objects before `receiveAndMerge()` applies particle ownership and
duplicate rules.

# Merge policy

`mergeWithParticle()` examines the incoming particle's parallel and movement
state and either inserts it, replaces a local copy, keeps it as a virtual copy,
or deletes it. The merge checks for a duplicate immediately before insertion
so the decision observes all particles accepted earlier in the same payload.

Particle identity and spatial duplication answer different questions. The
stable particle number identifies the physical particle, while
peano4::particles::particleIsDuplicate() detects two records at the same
position within peano4::particles::SpatialDuplicateTolerance. Removing a
spatial duplicate also releases its incoming particle object.

# Multiscale movement

Particle association follows the vertex and cell hierarchy described on
@ref page_peano4_particles_mesh_consistency. A particle can lift towards a
coarser cell, drop into a refined child, move through the sieve when its target
is not locally available, and cross an MPI rank boundary through the generated
exchange policy.

The particle state records whether the local copy is authoritative or virtual.
Movement actions update this state before the boundary merge decides which
copy survives.

# Copying

The generated copy constructor, copy assignment, and `clone()` create
independent particle objects. Boundary deserialisation also creates owning
incoming objects so a rejected record can be deleted safely.

*/
